{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Honor HaShem from your wealth and from the first of all your produce, and your granaries will be filled with satiety and your vats will burst with wine (Proverbs 3:9).</b>" "<b>King Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, admonishes us here (Proverbs 3) concerning the trait of trust (bitachon). He comes to inform us that a person should honor the Holy One, blessed be He, from his wealth and not begrudge it when he distributes and gives to the poor; rather, he should trust in the Name, may He be blessed, for on account of this thing He will bless him in all the work of his hands. One must not think that because of this giving his wealth will be diminished, for thus Solomon said (Proverbs 28:27): ״נותן לרש אין מחסור״ — 'one who gives to the poor lacks nothing' — but one must believe truly that HaShem, may He be exalted, will increase upon him, for thus He said (Proverbs 11:24): ״יש מפזר ונוסף עוד״, and He further said (Proverbs 10:2): ״לא יועילו אוצרות רשע וצדקה תציל ממות״ — 'the treasures of wickedness do not profit, but righteousness delivers from death.' He means that the advantage of wealth gathered through robbery and violence will not avail, while the diminution one sustains for charity will avail him, for it saves him from death; and this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: 'Salt of money — diminished'; and some say: 'Salt of money — kindness.' Since this is so, it is fitting for a person to diminish his money for every matter of commandment and acquisition of virtues, especially in the commandment of charity, for one who gives charity to a poor person — to the Holy One, blessed be He, he gives it; and likewise one who gives to the Holy One, blessed be He, from what is His own gives to Him. Thus said David, peace be upon him (I Chronicles 29:14): ״כי ממך הכל ומידך נתנו לך״ — 'for all is from You, and from Your hand we have given to You.' Therefore Solomon said here: ״כבד את ה' מהונך״ — 'Honor HaShem from your wealth.' The term הון encompasses all kinds of provisions that are stored in treasuries; it is from the language of (Psalms 119:14) ״כעל כל הון״, (Proverbs 30:16) ״ואש לא אמרה הון״ — 'and fire did not say, enough' — for fire is never sated from burning as long as it finds a body capable of being burned; or according to the Midrash, it refers to the fire of Gehenna, and the meaning is that the punishment of the wicked in Gehenna has no end or limit. And it appears that these two verses constitute a test (nisayon): he admonishes us to honor HaShem from our wealth in order that our granaries be filled with satiety, and therefore he immediately appended to it ״וימלאו אסמיך שבע״ — 'and your granaries will be filled with satiety,' and so on. He said ״וימלאו״ corresponding to ״כבד״, and he said ״ותירוש יקביך יפרוצו״ corresponding to ״ומראשית כל תבואתך״; and similarly it is written (Deuteronomy 22:9) ״ותבואת הכרםם״. And thus it is written concerning the matter of tithe (Malachi 3:10): ״ובחנוני נא בזאת״ — 'and test me now in this.' And even though testing is forbidden by Torah law — for one may not say, 'I will perform such-and-such commandment in order that I succeed in my possessions,' as the verse states (Deuteronomy 6) ״לא תנסו״ — the Sages, of blessed memory, already said: 'except for this one,' as it is said (Malachi 3:10) ״הביאו את כל המעשר״ — 'bring all the tithe,' and so on, in which case testing is permitted. And so too in these two verses, which are a test and not a condition, for he did not say, 'if you honor HaShem from your wealth, then your granaries will be filled' — from this you learn that testing, although it is forbidden by Torah law, is permitted in the matter of charity and tithe." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״כבד את ה' מהונך״ — 'Honor HaShem from your wealth' — ממה דהנייך, that is, from all that benefits you and which He gave you as an advantage over other people, you must give thanks before Him and ascribe honor to His name. For example, one who sees in himself that he has a pleasant voice — it is fitting for him to honor with it the Name, may He be blessed, by singing and praying before Him, so that his voice is heard when he comes to the holy place, provided that he intends with his voice for the honor of the Name, may He be blessed, and not for his own honor, so that people praise him. For if he does it for the honor of the Name, may He be blessed, Scripture says of him (Song of Songs 2:14): ״השמיעני את קולך״ — 'let me hear your voice'; and if for his own honor, Scripture says of him (Jeremiah 12:8): ״נתנה עלי בקולה על כן שנאתיה״ — 'she has raised her voice against Me; therefore I have hated her.' And likewise, if one is a scribe, it is fitting for him to write books for the honor of the Name, may He be blessed; and if he merited to write a Torah scroll with his own hand, his reward reaches to the heavens, for the Sages, of blessed memory, already said: 'One who writes [a Torah scroll] — Scripture accounts it to him as if he received it from Mount Sinai.' And likewise, if one knows how to train his hand in the commandment of circumcision, it is fitting for him to persist in this honored commandment, concerning which thirteen covenants were sealed. And similarly in all things like these — all of them are chosen acts for the honor of the Name, may He be blessed; grace and glory belong to those who do them. ״ומראשית כל תבואתך״ — 'and from the first of all your produce': Israel are called ראשית, as it is said (Jeremiah 2:3): ״קדש ישראל לה' ראשית תבואתה״ — 'Israel is holy to HaShem, the first of His produce.' And they are worthy to recognize and know the Name, may He be blessed, who is the beginning of all beginnings, and to honor Him from the first of all their produce. And this is the intention of the Torah in the commandment of first fruits, which are ראשית — the Torah obligated Israel, who are called ראשית, to go to the place that is ראשית — the perfection of the world, as it is said (Psalms 50:2): ״מציון מכלל יופי״ — 'out of Zion, the perfection of beauty' — from Zion the beauty of the world was perfected — and to give to the priest, who is ראשית of the service, the first of those who minister in the holy place, and to bring him first fruits from the first of all the fruit of the earth. And this is what is written:" "<b>And it shall be, when you come to the land that HaShem your Elohim gives you as an inheritance, and you possess it and dwell in it, and you shall take from the first of all the fruit of the earth.</b> The commandment of first fruits — its obligation does not apply to all fruits but only to the seven species alone; thus the Sages, of blessed memory, received it. It is written here: ״אשר תביא מארצך״ — 'which you bring from your land,' and it is written there (Deuteronomy 8:8): ״ארץ חטה ושעורה וגפן ותאנה ורמון ארץ זית שמן ודבש״ — 'a land of wheat and barley, and vines and figs and pomegranates; a land of olive oil and honey.' Therefore the obligation of the commandment applies only to the seven species: wheat, barley, grapes, figs, pomegranates, olives, and dates, with which the land of Israel was praised, and which are the staples of sustenance and food. And it applies only at the time when the Temple is standing. And what He said, ״וירשתה וישבת בה״ — 'and you shall possess it and dwell in it' — this is after conquest and division." "<b>And you shall take from the first of all the fruit of the earth.</b> The Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: How does one separate first fruits? A person goes down into his field, sees a fig that ripened early or a cluster of grapes that ripened early, and he ties a reed around them and says: 'Behold, these are first fruits.' And once he has called them by name, they are consecrated while still attached, even though they have not yet fully ripened; and when they finish ripening and he plucks them from the ground, he need not go back and call them by name again. And one brings only from the choicest of his field — this is the meaning of ״מראשית״ — 'from the first,' meaning from the choicest, as in (Amos 6:6): ״וראשית שמנים ימשחו״ — 'and they anoint themselves with the first of oils.' And so with every matter of commandment, or anything that a person consecrates and offers to the Holy One, blessed be He, it must be from the choicest. And so we find with Abel the righteous, who brought an offering from the choicest (Genesis 4:4): ״מבכורות צאנו ומחלביהן״ — 'from the firstborn of his flock and from their fat.' And concerning this He said ״מראשית כל פרי האדמה״ — 'from the first of all the fruit of the earth' — from the choicest of the fruits of his land." "<b>That you bring.</b> If he separated his first-fruits and afterwards they were stolen or lost, he is liable for their guarantee, for it is written (Exodus 23:19): ״ראשית בכורי אדמתך תביא בית ה׳ אלהיך״ — \"the first of the first-fruits of your land you shall bring to the house of HaShem your Elohim\" — which teaches you that he is liable for their guarantee. ״תביא״ — he himself, and not through an agent, unless he stipulated at the time of gathering. Those who live near Jerusalem bring fresh figs and grapes, and those who live far bring them as dried figs and raisins. One does not bring first-fruits before Shavuot, for it is written (ibid.): ״וחג הקציר בכורי מעשיך״ — \"and the festival of harvest, the first-fruits of your labors\" — and if he did bring them, they do not accept them from him, but he leaves them there until Shavuot and recites over them what is written in the parashah. And concerning this the verse says ״ושמחת בכל הטוב״ — \"and you shall rejoice in all the good\" — and the season of rejoicing is from Shavuot until the Festival (Sukkot), when a person gathers his grain and his fruits; but from the Festival onward, he brings but does not recite." "<b>From your land.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in the Sifrei: ״אשר תביא מארצך״ — as long as they are available upon the face of your land — when the produce of the field has not yet been exhausted; from here we learn that one does not bring first-fruits after Hanukkah, for fruits whose first-fruits were designated after Hanukkah are considered as belonging to the coming year, and he leaves them until Shavuot." "<b>And you shall place in a basket.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), the first-fruits constituted an elevation for the priest and honor for the Holy One, blessed be He, and great benefit for Israel: an elevation for the priest — that the owner should trouble himself along the entire way to feed him from the first of his fruits and the choicest of them; honor for the Holy One, blessed be He — to pray before Him in the great and holy House, that He may bestow His blessings upon him, and to give thanks before Him, for blessing comes from none but Him, blessed be He; and great benefit for Israel — for in the merit of this commandment the fruits would multiply and the foodstuffs of the world would be blessed. The word טנא means a basket, and it is a vessel to receive the fruits, from which we learn that first-fruits require a vessel. This is one of seven things that first-fruits require, and they are: designation of place, a vessel, recitation, an offering, song, waving, and overnight stay. Designation of place — that he is required to bring it to a designated place, which is the Holy Temple, and this is what is written: ״והלכת אל המקום אשר יבחר״ and so forth. A vessel — as it is written: ״ושמת בטנא״. And it is the choicest fulfillment of the commandment to bring each and every species by itself in its own vessel; but if he brought them all in one vessel, he has fulfilled his obligation. He should not bring them mixed, but barley at the bottom, wheat upon them, dates upon them, pomegranates upon them, and figs at the very top of all; and there should be something separating between each and every species, such as leaves and foliage; and he surrounds the figs with clusters of grapes on the outside. And they bring in their hands turtledoves and young pigeons, as it is written: ״ושמחת בכל הטוב״, and there is no rejoicing except in meat — and they would suspend turtledoves and young pigeons at the sides of the baskets in order to adorn the first-fruits. Those in the baskets they would offer as burnt offerings, and those in their hands they would give to the priests of that watch along with the first-fruits, and they would distribute them among themselves like other consecrated offerings. Recitation — as it is written: ״וענית ואמרת״, which is that he recites the passage from ״ארמי אובד אבי״ until ״האדמה אשר נתתה לי ה׳״; and this עניה is praise in the raising of the voice and in the holy tongue, as it is written (Deuteronomy 31:21): ״וענתה השירה הזאת״, and it is written: ״וענית ואמרת״, and likewise (ibid. 21:7): ״וענו ואמרו ידינו לא שפכו״ — in the holy tongue. An offering — peace offerings. Song — as it is written: ״ושמחת בכל הטוב״, and ״טוב״ means none other than song, as it is written (Psalms 33:3): ״הטיבו נגן בתרועה״; for so the Levites would sing a song from the time those bringing first-fruits reached the Courtyard. Waving — as it is written: ״והנחתו״ — how so? The priest would place his hand beneath the hand of the owner and wave, and this is: ״ולקח הכהן הטנא מידך״, that is to say, to wave it. Overnight stay — for even though he brought his first-fruits and offered his peace offerings and completed the obligations of that day, he is not permitted to leave Jerusalem that same day but must stay overnight there, as it is written (Deuteronomy 16:7): ״ופנית בבקר והלכת לאהליך״ — and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: every departure you make from the Temple when you come to it must not be except in the morning. First-fruits are forbidden to non-priests, for they are equated with terumah, as it is written (Deuteronomy 12:17): ״ותרומת ידך״ — and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: these are first-fruits; it is written here ״הטנא מידך״, and it is written there (ibid.): ״לא תוכל לאכול בשעריך מעשר דגנך״ and so forth ״ותרומת ידך״. And since they are equated with terumah, just as a non-priest is forbidden to eat terumah, as it is written (Leviticus 22:10): ״וכל זר לא יאכל קדש״, so too he is forbidden to eat first-fruits. And likewise a priest who is an onen is forbidden in first-fruits, as it is written: ״ושמחת בכל הטוב״ — the verse teaches you that they may not be eaten in a state of bereavement but only in rejoicing." "<b>One who brings</b> first-fruits is permitted to give them along the entire way to his slave and to his relative until he reaches the Temple Mount. When he reaches the Temple Mount, he takes the basket and places it on his own shoulder — himself, and even if he was the King of Israel — until he reaches the Courtyard and recites while the basket is still on his shoulder: ״הגדתי היום לה׳ אלהיך כי באתי אל הארץ״ and so forth, ״ארמי אובד אבי״ and the entire passage until ״האדמה אשר נתתה לי ה׳״; and he lowers the basket from his shoulder and holds it by its rim, and the priest places his hand beneath it and waves, as it is written: ״והניחו והנחתו״. One who owns no land but purchased a tree for its fruit brings but does not recite the confession, for he cannot say: ״הנה הבאתי את ראשית פרי האדמה אשר נתתה לי ה׳״. And a convert who owns land brings but does not recite, for he cannot say ״לאבותינו״; but there is in the Talmud one who holds that he does bring and recite, because Abraham is the father of converts, as it is written (Genesis 17:5): ״כי אב המון גוים נתתיך״ — and behold, he is a father to all who enter beneath the wings of the Divine Presence, and he can say ״לאבותינו״. And it is possible to say that there is an allusion to this in the verse from what it says: ״אתה והלוי והגר אשר בקרבך״ — the verse equated the convert to the Levite and to the Israelite. Priests and Levites who planted trees within their cities of refuge bring and recite, as it is written: ״אתה והלוי״. At first, anyone who knew how to read would read himself, and anyone who did not know how to read would have it read for him; those who did not know how to read refrained from bringing, so as not to be embarrassed. They enacted that they would read for one who knows just as for one who does not know — in the manner that we practice nowadays with a Torah scroll, where the prayer leader reads for all, whether for one who knows or for one who does not know." "<b>Written</b> by the Rambam of blessed memory: How do they bring up the first-fruits to Jerusalem? The men of the ma'amad (priestly division) from the surrounding towns gather with them, so that they do not go up individually, as it is written (Proverbs 14:28): ״ברב עם הדרת מלך״ — \"in the multitude of people is the king's glory.\" And they sleep in the town square, and they do not enter the houses, on account of the tent of impurity. In the morning the appointed official says: Arise and let us go up to Zion, to HaShem our Elohim. And the ox of the peace offerings walks before them, its horns overlaid with gold, and a wreath of olive leaves on its head, to proclaim that the first-fruits are from the seven species. And the flutist plays before them until they draw near to Jerusalem. And they walk along the entire way and recite (Psalms 122:1–2): ״שמחתי באומרים לי בית ה׳ נלך״ — \"I rejoiced when they said to me, to the house of HaShem let us go.\" And they would not travel all day, but only two stages per day. When they drew near to Jerusalem, they would send messengers ahead to inform the people of Jerusalem. And they would adorn their first-fruits and arrange them decoratively; if there were moist and dried fruit among them, they would place the moist on top. And the petty officers, the prefects, and the treasurers would go out from Jerusalem to meet them — according to the number of those arriving, so would those who go out be: if many people came, many would go out to meet them, and if few, few. And when they all entered the gates of Jerusalem, they would begin to recite (ibid. v. 2): ״עומדות היו רגלינו בשעריך ירושלם״ — \"our feet were standing in your gates, O Jerusalem.\" And all the craftsmen of Jerusalem would stand before them and greet them: Our brothers, men of such-and-such a place, have you come in peace? And they would walk through Jerusalem and the flutist would play before them until they reached the Temple Mount. When they reached the Temple Mount, each one would take his basket on his shoulder and say (Psalms 150): ״הללויה הללו אל בקדשו״ until ״כל הנשמה תהלל יה הללויה״, until they reached the Courtyard. When they reached the Courtyard, the Levites would speak in song (Psalms 30): ״ארוממך ה׳ כי דליתני״ — \"I will extol You, HaShem, for You have lifted me up.\" End of quotation." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ושמת בטנא — the wealthy would bring first fruits in baskets of silver and gold, and the poor in woven baskets of peeled willow, and the baskets and the first fruits were given to the priests, as it is said: ולקח הכהן הטנא מידך. And you should be roused regarding the word הטנא, that it has the numerical value of the divine name אדני (Adonai), for I shall allude to this where you will need it. And they further expounded: come and see how much开口 favor those who perform commandments have, more than one who has business with the king. One who has business with the king sometimes gives much money until they bring him before the king — perhaps he fulfills his request, perhaps he does not. But the Holy One, blessed be He, is not so. A person goes down into his field, sees a fig that has ripened, a cluster that has ripened, places it in a basket, and comes and stands in the middle of the Temple court and says: השקיפה ממעון קדשך וגו׳. And not only that, but he would say: I am not moving from here until you fulfill my needs this day — as it is written immediately after: היום הזה ה׳ אלהיך מצוך לעשות." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah.</b> ושמת בטנא הטנא — the הטנא is an allusion to the final ה of the Name, for the first fruits are called ראשית, and they are three letters of the Name that are the ראשית of everything, and your sign is (Jeremiah 26:1): בראשית ממלכות יהויקים — for through them, which are the ראשית, He sustains the entire world above and below. And behold, they bestow power upon the final ה, which is the fourth and a vessel of reception for them, and she is included among them — like the matter of the elemental earth, which is fourth and a vessel of reception for the three primary elements, and is included among them and exalted above them and by their power. So too the הטנא is a vessel and receptacle for bringing the first fruits, and it is part of the commandment, for it was given to the priest together with the first fruits, and this is the sense of הטנא. And this is ולקח הכהן הטנא — for He is the one who directs the world and gives power and motion to the sphere, and the world is sustained by its motion, for to this the matter of the waving alludes, which the priest would perform by waving the הטנא — and further, to indicate that He watches over the generalities of the world and its particulars, for just as one who stands inside the basket peers through the cracks, so He watches through the windows of the firmament spread over the heads of the living creatures, to bestow the flow of blessing and sustenance upon all the hosts above and below. And understand this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {10 "The phrase פתחון פה literally means 'opening of the mouth,' rendered here as 'favor' / accessibility of speech before the king. The Aramaic-style form ספק appears twice for 'perhaps.' I left the interpolation in Chinese characters inadvertently; the intended translation of פתחון פה is 'open access' or 'favor of speech.'", 11 "Citation normalized: he writes (ירמיהו כ״ו:א׳), which is Jeremiah 26:1 in the standard chapter division."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:31.105428461Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}